Experiment 1b:
Write a sample dart program to understand the language basics
void main() {
  // Variables and data types
  String name = "John Smith";
  int age = 25;
  double marks = 85.5;
  bool isStudent = true;

  // Display values
  print("Name: $name");
  print("Age: $age");
  print("Marks: $marks");
  print("Student: $isStudent");

  // Arithmetic operators
  int a = 10;
  int b = 5;

  print("Addition: ${a + b}");
  print("Subtraction: ${a - b}");
  print("Multiplication: ${a * b}");
  print("Division: ${a / b}");

  // If-else statement
  if (marks >= 40) {
    print("Result: Pass");
  } else {
    print("Result: Fail");
  }

  // For loop
  print("Numbers from 1 to 5:");
  for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    print(i);
  }

  // Function call
  greet(name);
}

// Function
void greet(String name) {
  print("Hello, $name!");
}

Experiment 1b:
Write a program to display “hello world” using flutter.

import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(const MyApp());
}

class MyApp extends StatelessWidget {
  const MyApp({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return const MaterialApp(
      debugShowCheckedModeBanner: false,
      home: Scaffold(
        body: Center(
          child: Text(
            'Hello, World!',
            style: TextStyle(
              fontSize: 30,
              fontWeight: FontWeight.bold,
            ),
          ),
        ),
      ),
    );
  }
}

Each Block of code:

import 'package:flutter/material.dart'; → Imports Flutter Material UI widgets.
main() → Starting point of the application.
runApp(const MyApp()); → Launches the Flutter app.
MyApp extends StatelessWidget → Creates a widget that does not maintain changing state.
MaterialApp → Provides the basic application structure.
Scaffold → Provides the screen layout.
Center → Places the content in the center.
Text → Displays Hello, World!
TextStyle → Makes the text 30 pixels and bold.

Brief Explanation
1. Import Flutter

import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

This imports Flutter's Material Design widgets.

It allows us to use widgets such as:

MaterialApp
Scaffold
Text
Center

2. Main function

void main() {

main() is the starting point of every Dart program.

When you run the Flutter application, execution starts here.

3. Start the Flutter application

runApp(const MyApp());

runApp() tells Flutter:

"Start my application using the MyApp widget."

MyApp() is the main widget we created below.

const means the widget can be created as a compile-time constant when its values don't change.

4. Create MyApp class

class MyApp extends StatelessWidget {

Here we create a class called MyApp.

StatelessWidget means the widget does not have changing state.

For example, a simple "Hello World" screen does not need to change.

5. Constructor

const MyApp({super.key});

This is the constructor for MyApp.

super.key passes the widget's key to the parent StatelessWidget.

For beginners, you can think of key as an identifier Flutter can use to identify a widget.

6. Build method

@override
Widget build(BuildContext context) {

build() tells Flutter what should appear on the screen.

@override means we are providing our own implementation of the build() method inherited from StatelessWidget.

BuildContext context gives Flutter information about where this widget is located in the widget tree.

7. Return MaterialApp

return const MaterialApp(

MaterialApp is the basic structure for a Flutter Material Design application.

It provides things such as:

App structure
Navigation
Themes
Material Design support

8. Remove debug banner
debugShowCheckedModeBanner: false,
Normally, Flutter displays a DEBUG banner in the top-right corner during development.
Setting it to false removes that banner.

9. Define the home screen
home: Scaffold(
home specifies the first screen of the application.
Scaffold provides the basic screen structure.

A Scaffold can contain:
appBar
body
drawer
floatingActionButton
bottomNavigationBar
Here, we only use body.

10. Create the body
body: Center(
body is the main content area of the screen.
Center places its child in the center of the screen.

11. Add Text
child: Text(
child means the widget contained inside another widget.
Here, the Center widget contains a Text widget.

12. Display the message
'Hello, World!',
This is the text that appears on the screen.
The output will be:

Hello, World!

13. Add text styling

style: TextStyle(

TextStyle controls how the text looks.

For example:

Font size
Font weight
Font color
Font family

14. Set font size

fontSize: 30,

This makes the text 30 pixels in size.

15. Make text bold

fontWeight: FontWeight.bold,

This makes Hello, World! appear in bold.

Understand the widget hierarchy

The most important concept for beginners is the widget tree:

MyApp
  ↓
MaterialApp
  ↓
Scaffold
  ↓
Center
  ↓
Text
  ↓
"Hello, World!"

Think of it like containers inside containers:

Application
   └── MaterialApp
        └── Scaffold
             └── Center
                  └── Text
Final output

The application displays:

              Hello, World!

The text is centered, bold, and 30 pixels in size.

Easy way to remember
Flutter code	Meaning
main()	Program starts here
runApp()	Starts Flutter app
StatelessWidget	Widget whose data doesn't change
build()	Defines what appears on screen
MaterialApp	Main application
Scaffold	Basic screen structure
body	Main screen content
Center	Centers its child
Text	Displays text
TextStyle	Changes text appearance
fontSize	Text size
fontWeight	Text thickness
